<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artsakh (historical province)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artsakh was a province of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, corresponding broadly to the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>. After the partition of Armenia between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> around 387 it passed with neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utik">Utik</a> to Caucasian Albania, but its population remained closely tied to Armenian culture and the Armenian Church. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was ruled by Armenian princely houses such as that of Khachen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artsakh-historical-province.jpg" alt="Artsakh (historical province)" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original author: User Hovik95, I colored Artsakh green. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artsakh_within_Armenian_Kingdom.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Artsakh lay on the easternmost edge of the Armenian Plateau, in the eastern part of the Lesser Caucasus, and was mostly mountainous and forested. Its area is estimated at 11,528 square kilometres. It bordered the Armenian provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utik">Utik</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gardman">Gardman</a> to the north-east and Syunik to the south-west, and the Arax river formed its southern boundary. Its two largest rivers, the Gargar and the Tartar, flow east to join the Kura. Important places, mostly fortified towns, included Parisos, Tigranakert, Sodk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>, and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the castle of Khachen served for a long time as the centre of Artsakh. According to the 7th-century Ashkharatsoyts, the province had twelve cantons.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>'s &quot;Orchistene&quot;, a region that supplied the most cavalry of the Armenian provinces, is believed to be a rendering of the name, and some scholars identify Artsakh with lands named in the inscriptions of Urartian kings. Many etymologies have been proposed, among them a derivation from a word for trees or bushes, from the Armenian word for grapevine, and from a root meaning rocky or mountainous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Historians disagree on how early Artsakh belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Robert Hewsen holds that it joined only after 189 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a> began to expand the Armenian kingdom, while many Armenian historians argue that it was ruled and populated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> from the formation of the Armenian people. The early Armenian historian Movses Khorenatsi traces its ruling families to Aran, a descendant of the patriarch Hayk. After Armenia's conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 301, Artsakh became a stronghold for missionaries, and around 410 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesrop-mashtots">Mesrop Mashtots</a> opened the first Armenian school at Amaras.</p><p>By the Peace of Acilisene in 387 the Armenian kingdom was partitioned between the Roman and Sasanian empires, and Caucasian Albania, then a Sasanian ally, received Artsakh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gardman">Gardman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utik">Utik</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-avarayr">Battle of Avarayr</a> in 451 it became a centre of resistance to Sasanian Iran.</p><p>From the 7th to the 9th century the Arab caliphates dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>. In the early 9th century the Armenian princes Sahl Smbatian and Esayi Abu-Muse revolted against Arab rule and set up the principalities of Khachen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dizak">Dizak</a>, and in 852 to 855 they fought the Abbasid commander Bugha, who failed 28 times to take Ktich Castle. From about 1000 the rulers of Khachen styled themselves Kings of Albania or of Artsakh, and later the principality split into smaller parts, the Khamsa Melikdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>. Artsakh then became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of successive Turkic and Iranian powers, until it was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artsakh (historical province)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
